GameLib
=======

A library of JavaScript modules for writing games: a fixed-rate main
loop, drawing into a block of pixels, text, keys and gamepad, a sound
synthesiser, saved scores and options. !Pacman is built on it, and
your own games can use it too. It is not a program: double-clicking
it shows this text.

Where it is
-----------
It lives in $.!Boot.Resources.!GameLib, with the other system
resources. At start-up the desktop runs its !Boot file, which sets
two system variables (*Show GameLib$* lists them):

  GameLib$Dir      the library's directory
  GameLib$Path     where programs look for its modules: a list of
                   directories, each ending in '.', with commas
                   between them (normally just <GameLib$Dir>.)

and GameLib$Version. A variable that is already set is kept.

Using it in a program
---------------------
A JavaScript program (file type JSScript) imports a module by its
name after 'gamelib/', wherever the program is:

  import { Loop } from 'gamelib/Loop';
  import { Surface, rgb } from 'gamelib/Surface';

The name is looked for in each directory of GameLib$Path in turn
(as Name, then Name/js); the first one found is used. If it is in
none of them, GameLib$Dir is tried last. 'gamelib' may be in any
case; a module name is letters, digits, _ and -, with / between
directories, so an import cannot reach outside the library.

From the command line or an Obey file the files are, for example,
<GameLib$Dir>.Loop, or GameLib:Loop.

Using your own copy
-------------------
To try a changed module without touching this one, put it in a
directory of your own and add that directory to the front of the
path, after the desktop has started:

  Set GameLib$Path RAM::RamDisc0.$.MyLib.,<GameLib$Dir>.

Programs started after that find your module first. The library's
modules import each other by relative names, so a changed inner
module is not seen by the others that import it: copy a module with
the files it imports. GameLib$Path is stored with <GameLib$Dir>
filled in, so changing GameLib$Dir later has no effect until
GameLib$Path is set again.

The modules
-----------
  Maths      wrap, clamp, lerp; Rng, a seedable random generator
  Loop       a fixed-rate game loop (60 ticks a second, say) on the
             browser's animation frames
  Surface    a block of pixels to draw into; rgb makes a colour
  Raster     filling triangles and boxes a row at a time (used by
             Shapes)
  Shapes     discs, pies, arcs, lines, polygons, rounded boxes
  Font       the 8 x 8 system font, drawn onto a Surface, with
             shadow, outline and scale
  Display    shows a Surface, full screen or in a window, scaled to
             fit with sharp pixels
  Keys       the keyboard as named actions: held, pressed, latest
  Pad        the first gamepad, as named presses
  Options    a game's settings, described by a list
  ScoreTable a high score table, kept sorted
  Choices    where a game keeps its saved files (in Choices$Write)
  Audio      plays a game's synthesised sounds with Web Audio
  Synth      a small sound synthesiser working on arrays of samples
  SynthEnv   the envelopes, sweeps and notes that drive Synth
  SynthFx    filter, echo, fade and other finishing touches

Everything but Display, Audio and Surface.present works without a
browser, and none of the modules reaches into the desktop itself.
Each one says at its top what it offers: load it into !Edit.

The guide $.Docs.GameLib says more, with a small game as an example.
